          "title": "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "78119916-85ee-569a-8774-5fcca61d54dc",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Let $C$ be the straight segment from $A=(2,3)$ to $B=(2,2)$. Let $f(x,y)=e^{x+y}$. Which definite integral is equivalent to $\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$? Do not evaluate the integral.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The endpoint position vectors give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(t)&=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)\\\\\n&=\\langle 2,- t + 3\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ and $t=1$ this gives $A=(2,3)$ and $B=(2,2)$, respectively.\n\nDifferentiate, compute the speed, and substitute both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 0,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-1)^2}=1,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=e^{(2)+(- t + 3)}\\\\\n&=e^{- t + 5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the scalar arc-length formula,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(e^{- t + 5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
            {
              ":answer-field/id": "e9666430-37fb-5b30-92e0-f2856748c4de",
              ":answer-field/key": "selection",
              ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
