        "question": {
          ":question/id": "186de298-0dab-5c10-83c8-242fd0c308ab",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "![Segment C from A(3,1) to B(5,-1).](segment-v2-dfa2bcd331bca4361529.png)\n\nThe figure gives the endpoints of the straight segment $C$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Let $f(x,y)=e^{x+y}$. Use the parameterization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u)=\\langle - u + 6,u - 2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nDetermine its parameter interval from the endpoints. Which definite integral, with increasing limits and parameter $u$, is equivalent to $\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$? Do not evaluate the integral.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The endpoints are $A=(3,1)$ and $B=(5,-1)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(1)&=\\langle 5,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf r(3)&=\\langle 3,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $1\\le u\\le 3$ covers the segment from $B$ to $A$. Reversing traversal does not negate a scalar arc-length integral; its speed is positive.\n\nDifferentiate, compute the speed, and substitute both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(u)&=\\langle -1,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(1)^2}=\\sqrt{2},\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(u))&=e^{(- u + 6)+(u - 2)}\\\\\n&=e^{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the scalar arc-length formula,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}f(\\mathbf r(u))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}u,\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{2}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(e^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          ":question/answer-fields": [
